- src/services/contentstack.ts :: options — This is the Contentstack Delivery SDK Stack initialization config. It currently lacks a live_preview block. Adding live_preview here enables the SDK to fetch draft/preview content. The Stack instance exported on line 91 is what live-preview-utils will wrap. [existing helper: Stack]
- src/context/contentstackContext.tsx :: ContentstackContext — This is the React context that carries all CMS content to the component tree. The onEntryChange callback from live-preview-utils must update the value of this context so that every consumer of useContent re-renders with draft content. The context value is currently a static object — it needs to beco [existing helper: ContentstackContext]
- src/pages/_app.tsx :: MyApp — This is the root App component for the Pages Router. It is the correct place to initialize @contentstack/live-preview-utils on the client side (ContentstackLivePreview.init) and to wire the onEntryChange callback that will push draft content updates into ContentstackContext, causing re-renders throu [existing helper: useContent]
- .env.local.sample — This file documents the required environment variables for developers. New variables needed for Live Preview (CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN, CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST, CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_ENABLED) must be documented here so the team can configure preview environments.
- src/hooks/useContent.ts :: useContent — This is the shared hook that 20+ components use to read CMS content from ContentstackContext. It is the downstream pipeline that onEntryChange must ultimately trigger — when the context value updates (via onEntryChange), useContent's useContext(ContentstackContext) will return the new draft data, an [existing helper: getContentByKey]

STORY CONTEXT:
Title: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS
Description: AS a Content Author, I WANT to preview draft entries in CMS, SO THAT I can see how content will be shown on the website. This requires integrating Contentstack's Live Preview SDK for a Next.js CSR (Pages Router) application: adding live_preview config to the Stack initialization, installing and initializing @contentstack/live-preview-utils on the client, wiring an onEntryChange callback to re-render with draft content through the existing ContentstackContext/useContent pipeline, and documenting the required preview environment variables. Limitation: Might not be possible to preview protected pages and content.


